---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Counter Offer Email Generator (Free) — Salary Counter Template | SalaryPitcher"
  description="Use our free counter offer email generator to build a professional salary counter offer email in minutes. Proven template, market data, no signup."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Counter Offer Email Generator", path: "/counter-offer-email-template" },
  ]}
>
  <!-- FAQPage Schema JSON-LD -->
  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is a counter offer email template?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A counter offer email template is a structured message you send to a recruiter or hiring manager after receiving a job offer, politely asking for a higher salary, bonus, or improved benefits while explaining why you deserve it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much should I counter in the email?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most successful counter offers ask for 10-20% above the initial offer, anchored to market data for the specific role and location. If the offer is well below market, you can justify a higher counter with wage data."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I send a counter offer email or call?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Email is usually better: it gives you a written record, prevents emotional mistakes, and lets you present market data and achievements exactly as planned. You can follow up with a call if needed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the counter offer email generator really free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. No login, no credit card, no hidden limits. Answer a few questions and get a personalized counter offer email you can copy and send."
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  </script>

  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto w-full space-y-8">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="text-center space-y-4 py-4">
      <div
        class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-mono font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider bg-accent-blue/15 text-accent-blue border border-accent-blue/10"
      >
        Free Template + Generator
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight">
        Free Counter Offer Email Generator
      </h1>
      <p class="text-sm text-body max-w-xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
        Turn a generic counter offer email template into a personalized message
        in minutes. This free generator fills in the blanks — market range, your
        achievement, and your target number — so your email sounds written for
        you, not copied from the internet.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/pitch-assistant"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-8 py-3.5 bg-ink text-canvas hover:bg-ink/90 font-medium rounded-full text-sm transition-all duration-300 cursor-pointer ripple-btn btn-press"
      >
        Generate My Counter Offer Email Free →
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Anatomy of the template -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        The Anatomy of a Counter Offer Email Template
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        Every effective counter offer email follows the same four-part
        structure. Whether you write it yourself or use the generator, make sure
        these sections are present:
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">1. Thank the employer</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Open with genuine enthusiasm for the role and the team. This signals
            good faith and keeps the conversation collaborative.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">2. State your number with data</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Name a specific target salary and back it with a market range for
            the role in that location. Objective data turns a request into a
            business case.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">3. Tie your achievement to the ask</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Reference one concrete result — revenue grown, time saved, a project
            shipped — that explains why you are worth the higher number.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">4. Close flexibly</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Invite a conversation and leave room on non-salary items like bonus,
            equity, or start date. Ultimatums end negotiations; flexibility
            keeps them moving.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Sample template -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        Counter Offer Email Sample Template
      </h2>
      <div
        class="p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-md font-sans text-xs text-ink space-y-3 leading-relaxed"
      >
        <p>Subject: [Role] Offer — [Your Name]</p>
        <p>Hi [Hiring Manager],</p>
        <p>
          Thank you so much for the offer to join [Company] as [Role]. I really
          enjoyed meeting the team and am excited about the work ahead.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before finalizing, I wanted to discuss the base salary. Typical market
          ranges for this role in [Location] are between [Low] and [High], and
          based on my experience plus the results I plan to deliver — including
          [key achievement] — I would be comfortable at [Target Number].
        </p>
        <p>
          I am flexible on start date and happy to talk through any other part
          of the package. Are you open to exploring this?
        </p>
        <p>Best,<br />[Your Name]</p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
        Rather than fill in the blanks by hand, let the
        <a
          href="/pitch-assistant"
          class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
          >counter offer email generator</a
        >
        build the full draft for you with your actual role, market, and
        achievement data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- When to send -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        When and How to Send a Counter Offer Email
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        Timing matters as much as wording. Send your counter within 24-48 hours
        of receiving the written offer, after you have had time to review the
        full package — base, bonus, equity, benefits, and PTO. Responding too
        slowly signals low interest; responding too fast signals you did not
        think it through.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        If the offer you received is well below market, see our guide on
        <a
          href="/guides/how-to-counter-lowball-offer"
          class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
          >how to counter a lowball offer</a
        >
        for the exact phrasing and strategy. And before you send anything,
        double-check the common pitfalls in our list of
        <a
          href="/guides/salary-negotiation-mistakes"
          class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
          >salary negotiation mistakes to avoid</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Tone -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        Choosing the Right Tone for Your Counter Offer Email
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        The same template can sound completely different depending on the tone
        you choose. Match the tone to your situation:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Confident and polite</strong> — the default
          for most situations. Direct about the number, warm about the role,
          and easy for a recruiter to say yes to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Assertive</strong> — best when you hold real
          leverage, like a competing offer or a rare skill set. Firm on the
          number, still professional in tone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Warm and collaborative</strong> — suited to
          small teams and startups where the relationship matters as much as
          the number. Softer framing, same market-backed ask.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        The generator lets you pick the tone and polish the result after it is
        written, so you can try an assertive version and a warmer version side
        by side before choosing. Avoid the tone mistakes that cost candidates
        offers — see our
        <a
          href="/guides/salary-negotiation-mistakes"
          class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
          >10 salary negotiation mistakes to avoid</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- After sending -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        What to Do After You Send the Counter Offer Email
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
            Give them 48-72 hours before following up
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Compensation decisions often need approval above the recruiter.
            Sending a nudge sooner than 48 hours reads as pressure.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
            Keep the door open on the full package
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            If base salary comes back firm, pivot to bonus, equity, or PTO
            rather than ending the conversation. The
            <a
              href="/guides/how-to-negotiate-sign-on-bonus"
              class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
              >sign-on bonus guide</a
            >
            covers the most flexible lever.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink">
            Confirm the final offer in writing
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Once you agree, ask for the revised offer letter before you sign or
            give notice. Verbal agreements can shift during formal approval.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
        For the full step-by-step process from offer to accepted counter, read
        <a
          href="/guides/how-to-negotiate-salary-after-job-offer"
          class="text-accent-blue hover:underline font-semibold"
          >how to negotiate salary after a job offer</a
        >.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section
      class="p-6 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-between items-center gap-6"
    >
      <div class="space-y-1 text-center sm:text-left">
        <p class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
          Skip the blank spaces — get a full draft.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-mute">
          The free generator writes your counter offer email for you in under
          two minutes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a
        href="/pitch-assistant"
        class="px-6 py-2.5 bg-ink text-canvas hover:bg-ink/90 font-medium rounded-full text-xs transition-colors shrink-0 ripple-btn btn-press"
      >
        Generate Counter Offer Email →
      </a>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-5">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink tracking-tight">
        Counter Offer Email Generator — FAQ
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">
            What is a counter offer email template?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            A structured message you send to a recruiter or hiring manager after
            receiving a job offer, politely requesting a higher salary, bonus,
            or improved benefits with a clear rationale.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">
            How much should I counter in the email?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Most successful counters ask for 10-20% above the initial offer,
            anchored to market data for the specific role and location.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">
            Should I send a counter offer email or call?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Email is usually better: it gives you a written record, prevents
            emotional mistakes, and lets you present your case exactly as
            planned.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-ink mb-1">
            Is the counter offer email generator really free?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Yes. No login, no credit card, no hidden limits.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
